Setup. For a real parameter α ∈ (0,1) and a length λ > 0, the middle‑α Cantor set with first construction interval [0,λ] is the compact set K(α,λ) ⊂ [0,λ] obtained by the usual iterative construction in which, at each stage, the open middle subinterval of relative length α is removed from every remaining closed interval (so each closed interval of length ℓ is replaced by two closed intervals of length ℓ(1−α)/2 separated by an open gap of length αℓ).

Let (α_i)_{i∈ℕ} ⊂ (0,1) and (λ_i)_{i∈ℕ} ⊂ (0,∞) be null sequences (α_i → 0 and λ_i → 0), and for each i set K_i := K(α_i, λ_i). Define the sumset
   S := { ∑_{i=1}^∞ x_i : x_i ∈ K_i for every i }
(assumed convergent, e.g. when ∑ λ_i < ∞), and for each z ∈ ℕ define the tail set
   R_z := { ∑_{i=z}^∞ x_i : x_i ∈ K_i for every i ≥ z }.

Recall that a Cantorval is a nonempty compact subset of ℝ that is the closure of its interior, has no isolated points, and possesses both nondegenerate components and uncountably many components that are not isolated; one further distinguishes:
 • an M‑Cantorval — every nondegenerate component has both sides accumulated by other nondegenerate components;
 • an L‑Cantorval — every nondegenerate component has its left side, but not its right side, accumulated by other nondegenerate components;
 • an R‑Cantorval — symmetric mirror condition on the right side.

Problem. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on the parameter sequence (α_i)_{i∈ℕ} and on the tail sets (R_z)_{z∈ℕ} under which the sumset S is, respectively, an M‑Cantorval, an L‑Cantorval, or an R‑Cantorval.

